  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 AM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nCanadian high pressure has temporarily displaced a towering \n500-mb ridge to our west for the remainder of the Labor Day \nweekend. Our coolest day of the week appears to be today with \nhigh temperatures generally in the upper-70s; perhaps 80 degrees\ntoward Lafayette. High pressure promotes dry conditions through\nat least Wednesday morning but we will see a subtle warming \ntrend beyond today as return southerly flow, and the resumption \nof upper-level ridging, eventually nudges temperatures back \ntoward 90 degrees. Wednesday, those south of the US 24 corridor \nhave a 25-30% chance of seeing a 90-degree temperature per\nglobal ensembles.\n\nTo our north midweek, low pressure is wrapping up over western \nOntario with an occluded/cold front dropping south into the Midwest. \nMoisture return into this front is lackluster and the front stalls \natop the now-depressed upper-level ridge. This results in \nfairly modest rain chances at 20-40% Wednesday through Thursday \nmorning. Beyond this, there is growing concensus that a zonal \nbut progressive upper-air pattern takes shape presenting passing\nchances for rain and storms while pushing temperatures near to\nslightly above normal (normals of 77/54 by late next week).",
